course: dictionary
lesson: 7032
title: Tiny bits called particles
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: A particle is a very small piece or bit of something. «The sunlight showed dust particles floating in the room.» Each one is too small to see on its own, but you can see many together.
explain: In science, particles are the tiny bits that all matter is made of, such as atoms and molecules. Matter is anything that takes up space, like water, air, wood or your own body. «Electricity is a flow of tiny charged particles through a material.»
explain: Context helps you here too. In «In a liquid like juice the particles stay close but slide past each other», the words stay close and slide past tell you what particles do. Try the word bits in the sentence instead of particles. If it still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: A particle is a very small piece or bit of something.
fact: In science, particles are the tiny bits that all matter is made of, such as atoms and molecules.
fact: In a solid, particles are packed tightly and vibrate in place.
fact: In a gas, particles are far apart and move fast, and heating makes them move even faster.
fact: In grammar, a particle is a small word, like «up» in «give up».
exercise: «The room was full of dust particles.» What is a particle? | A loud noise in the air | *A very small piece or bit of something | A large machine that heats water
exercise: «In a liquid like juice the particles stay close but slide past each other.» What do the particles do in a liquid? | *They stay close but slide past each other | They are packed tightly and do not move | They fly far apart and never meet
exercise: In the phrase «give up», which word is the particle? | *up | give | the
test: In a gas, how far apart are the particles? | Packed tightly in rows | *Far apart | Very close together | Stuck to the walls
test: When you heat something, what happens to its particles? | *They move faster | They move slower | They disappear | They stop moving
exam: According to «Cold gas particles move slower and hit the walls less hard», why do tyres have lower pressure in cold weather? | *Because the particles hit the walls less hard | Because the tyres are made of particles that melt | Because drivers stop checking them | Because the particles get bigger
